[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a due/overdue reminder reach you? → In-app badge + panel (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m In-app only — so no [38;5;153mNotification[39m API, no permission handling, no service worker. The system is now genuinely small,
  which makes the last structural question the one about state.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a reminder is a [3mthing we store[0m or just [3ma view over your tasks[0m.

  - [1mPurely derived[0m — the panel is a live filter: "tasks with a due date in the next N hours, or already past." Nothing
    new is persisted; there are no notification records to get stale, no cleanup when you complete a task or move a due
    date, no possibility of the badge disagreeing with the list. The cost: there's nothing to dismiss. An overdue task
    nags until you complete it or reschedule it.
  - [1mSnooze as a field on the task[0m — still derived, but each task carries a [38;5;153mremindAfter[39m timestamp. "Snooze 1 hour" writes
    one field and the task drops out of the filter until then. Adds one field and one action, keeps the
    no-stale-records property.
  - [1mReal notification records[0m — each reminder becomes a stored object with seen/dismissed/snoozed state. Full
    flexibility, but now there are two sources of truth to keep in sync, and every task mutation needs to reconcile its
    notifications. This is where the bugs live in systems this size.

  Reversibility: derived → snooze-field is a trivial addition later. Derived → stored records is a rewrite of the
  notification layer, so it's the one worth deciding deliberately.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the snooze field. It's one extra property on the task for the only dismissal behavior that actually
  matters, and it keeps tasks as the single source of truth.
